KNOWNBUG libstdcxx
main.cpp
--cpp14 --object-bits 12 --unwind 8 --no-unwinding-assertions
^\[main\.assertion\.1\] line \d+ init-capture by move: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.2\] line \d+ WRONG \(must FAIL\): FAILURE$
^\*\* 1 of \d+ failed
^VERIFICATION FAILED$
^EXIT=10$
^SIGNAL=0$
--
^warning: ignoring
^CONVERSION ERROR$
--
g++ and clang++ (GCC 16 for C++23 and C++26) accept this program, and it fails
only its WRONG assertion.  [expr.prim.lambda.capture]/6: an init-capture `q =
std::move(p)' declares a member initialized as `auto q = std::move(p)', by
unique_ptr's move constructor, which leaves p null.  CBMC initializes the
member without calling a constructor (a class with counting constructors shows
neither its copy nor its move constructor runs), so p and the closure's member
both own the int, and the second destructor reports "double delete".  A plain
capture by copy calls the copy constructor.
